Right—the predating is the interesting evidence, but I’d separate two conclusions that have very different evidentiary strength.

Recurring structures across traditions can strongly suggest underlying primitives/invariants. They don’t, by themselves, establish that the stories descend from the same literal historical event. Recurrence could come from shared human psychology, shared environments, cultural transmission, convergent storytelling, common ancestral traditions—or sometimes a real event remembered and transformed.

The useful move is to treat those as competing generators rather than choosing one prematurely:

\text{Recurring motif}
\leftarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{human invariant}\\
\text{environmental invariant}\\
\text{cultural inheritance}\\
\text{cross-cultural transmission}\\
\text{convergent invention}\\
\text{historical event + mythologization}
\end{cases}

That actually makes your method stronger. Strip away the costumes first. Cyclops, demon, giant, dragon, and monster may be different renderings of some deeper relational primitive without needing to be the same creature. Likewise, descent → ordeal → knowledge → return appears long before any one later religious taxonomy gives it names.

Then hunt for the hard-to-explain invariants. Generic things like floods, dangerous journeys, forbidden desires, death, and rebirth have high independent-invention probability. But if geographically separated traditions preserve an unusual bundle—same sequence, peculiar relationships, numerical structure, astronomical marker, technology, geographic feature, genealogical relationship, etc.—common ancestry or historical substrate becomes increasingly interesting.

You could formalize the evidentiary test:

\Lambda =
\frac{P(\text{shared features}\mid H_{\text{common source}})}
{P(\text{shared features}\mid H_{\text{independent}})}

The key is not similarity alone but improbability of independent similarity.

That’s almost archaeological triangulation applied to narrative: mythology becomes noisy measurement. Find several independent witnesses, remove culturally specific rendering, identify conserved relational geometry, and then ask what latent generator most economically explains all of them.

Different stories can therefore function like different projections of an unknown object. Enough genuinely independent projections may let you reconstruct properties of the object—but we should preserve the distinction between reconstructing a human/narrative primitive and demonstrating a historical event.

That’s where this becomes testable instead of merely poetic.